<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     6. STRATEGIC RECOMMENDATIONS
════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="recommendations">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-num">6</div>
    <h2>Strategic Recommendations</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-grid">
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 1 — High Priority</div>
      <h4>Reassess Deep-Discount Strategy (&gt;60%) for Low-End SKUs</h4>
      <p>Products discounted 60–80% average 4.03–4.06 stars, lower than products discounted under 30% (4.17 avg). Discounting is not converting to satisfaction — it may be attracting price-sensitive buyers with higher return rates and complaint rates. Reduce aggressive discounting on sub-₹500 products where quality concerns are most frequent.</p>
      <div class="data-point">Supporting data: r = −0.156 (disc% vs rating); 81–100% disc bracket averages 3.98 ★ vs 4.17 ★ for &lt;30% disc</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 2 — High Priority</div>
      <h4>Prioritize Assortment Expansion in Office Products</h4>
      <p>With only 31 products but the highest average rating (4.31) and lowest average discount (12%), Office Products punches well above its weight on customer satisfaction. Its low engagement metrics (149K total reviews) signal untapped scale opportunity. Growing this category could improve catalog-wide satisfaction with minimal promotional spend.</p>
      <div class="data-point">Supporting data: Office Products avg rating 4.31 at 12% avg discount vs catalog avg 4.10 at 47.7%</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 3 — Medium Priority</div>
      <h4>Audit the Headphones &amp; Earbuds Sub-Category for Assortment Quality</h4>
      <p>66 products in this sub-category average 3.93 stars — the lowest among large Electronics sub-groups — despite carrying the second-highest average discount (59%). Negative sentiment themes (noise, fake, defective) cluster here. An assortment quality audit should identify and delist underperforming SKUs, then replace with better-reviewed alternatives.</p>
      <div class="data-point">Supporting data: Headphones avg rating 3.93 ★; 59% avg discount; "noise" appears in 37 review titles, "fake" in 13</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 4 — Medium Priority</div>
      <h4>Leverage High-Engagement Electronics Products for Social Proof</h4>
      <p>AmazonBasics HDMI cables and boAt Bassheads earphones each exceed 363K reviews at strong 4.1–4.4 ratings. These products represent proven anchors with massive social proof. Feature them prominently in category pages and promotional bundles. Their review volumes signal organic demand that doesn't require heavy discounting.</p>
      <div class="data-point">Supporting data: Top 6 most-reviewed products all in Electronics, 313K–427K reviews each, ratings 4.1–4.4</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 5 — Medium Priority</div>
      <h4>Focus Product Improvement Investment on Functional Reliability</h4>
      <p>Review sentiment analysis shows "working," "fast charging," and "easy" are the primary positive drivers — not aesthetics or features. Customer disappointment centers on quality consistency, defects, and unmet functional expectations. Vendor quality standards should be tightened for categories below 4.0 avg rating, with particular focus on Heating/Cooling products (3.99 avg) and Printers &amp; Inks (3.96 avg).</p>
      <div class="data-point">Supporting data: "working" (243), "bad" (109), "issue" (70), "defective" (21) in review titles; Heating/Cooling avg 3.99 ★</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-num">Recommendation 6 — Ongoing</div>
      <h4>Protect Margin on Premium (&gt;₹5,000) Products</h4>
